Concurrency / Race Conditions

TC-15: Same User Sends Two Gift Requests Simultaneously
Category: Concurrency
Priority: P0
Preconditions:
User authenticated.
Wallet balance = exactly enough for one transaction only.
Example: balance = 5000 coins.
Gift cost = 5000 coins.
Steps:
Send two identical requests at the exact same time using different threads/tools.
Both requests use different idempotency keys.
Expected Result:
Only one request succeeds.
The second request fails due to insufficient balance.
Final balance is never negative.
Only one transaction exists in database.
Why it matters:
Detects double-spending issues caused by race conditions.

TC-16: Concurrent Requests from Two Devices Using Same Account
Category: Concurrency
Priority: P0
Preconditions:
User logged in on two devices.
Wallet balance supports only one transaction.
Steps:
Device A sends gift request.
Device B sends gift request simultaneously.
Expected Result:
Database row lock prevents balance corruption.
Wallet balance remains accurate.
No duplicate coin deduction occurs.
Why it matters:
Verifies row-level locking on wallet records.

TC-17: Wallet Top-Up Webhook Arrives During Gift Processing
Category: Concurrency
Priority: P0
Preconditions:
User wallet balance is low.
Payment provider webhook can add coins.
Steps:
Start gift transaction.
During processing, trigger wallet top-up webhook.
Expected Result:
Final balance calculation remains correct.
No lost update occurs.
Transaction remains consistent.
Why it matters:
Detects synchronization issues between payments and spending.

TC-18: Audience Changes During Winner Selection
Category: Concurrency
Priority: P1
Preconditions:
Room contains multiple viewers.
Steps:
Start gift request.
Several viewers leave or join the room during execution.
Expected Result:
Winner selection remains valid.
No duplicate winners returned.
Winners count remains within expected limit.
Why it matters:
Detects race conditions in audience snapshot handling.
Idempotency

TC-19: Retry Same Request Using Same idempotency_key
Category: Idempotency
Priority: P0
Preconditions:
Successful transaction already completed.
Steps:
Send valid request.
Retry exactly the same request using the same idempotency_key.
Expected Result:
No new transaction is created.
Wallet is not charged again.
API returns original transaction result or appropriate duplicate response.
Why it matters:
Prevents duplicate charges caused by mobile network retries.

TC-20: Reuse Same idempotency_key with Different Payload
Category: Idempotency
Priority: P0
Preconditions:
Previous request already used the idempotency key.
Steps:
Submit request with idempotency_key X.
Submit another request with same key X but different quantity.
Expected Result:
Request rejected.
No additional transaction created.
Wallet remains unchanged.
Why it matters:
Prevents replay attacks and transaction tampering.
